<section id="interactive-gameplay" class="level2" data-number="4">
<h2 data-number="4" class="anchored" data-anchor-id="interactive-gameplay"><span class="header-section-number">4</span> Interactive Gameplay</h2>
<p>The game runs in Jupyter notebooks with an interactive widget-based interface. Players click cells to make their moves as ⭕, while the AI responds as ❌.</p>
<p>The AI is truly optimal - the best you can do is force a draw. Give it a try and see if you can achieve that!</p>
</section>
<section id="key-features" class="level2" data-number="5">
<h2 data-number="5" class="anchored" data-anchor-id="key-features"><span class="header-section-number">5</span> Key Features</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Optimal play</strong>: The AI never makes mistakes</li>
<li><strong>Fast computation</strong>: Magic square logic speeds up win detection</li>
<li><strong>Interactive UI</strong>: Click-based gameplay using ipywidgets</li>
<li><strong>Educational</strong>: Clear implementation of classic AI concepts</li>
</ul>
<p>The complete code is available on my <a href="https://github.com/MahbubAlam231">GitHub</a> with detailed comments explaining each component.</p>
</section>
<section id="what-i-learned" class="level2" data-number="6">
<h2 data-number="6" class="anchored" data-anchor-id="what-i-learned"><span class="header-section-number">6</span> What I Learned</h2>
<p>This project reinforced several important concepts:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li>How simple mathematical tricks (magic squares) can optimize algorithms</li>
<li>The elegance of recursive game tree exploration</li>
<li>The importance of assuming optimal play in competitive AI</li>
<li>Building interactive experiences in Jupyter notebooks</li>
</ol>
<p>Next, I’m planning to extend this approach to more complex games like Connect Four or even Chess endgames!</p>


</section>

